    <dc:description>Articles in this issue:

Politics

Business

Cartoon: The real threat to global growth

The man who would change Russia

Two cheers for Trump Accounts

England needs fewer council homes, not more

A no-brainer for protecting your brain

Who is capable of evil?

Why are big AI labs hiring so many philosophers?

Prepare for a world of ad hoc coalitions

Medicine can treat obesity, but can’t agree on what obesity is

How dementia is being defeated

How realistic is Andy Burnham’s council-house building pledge?

Britain’s social housing subsidises the wrong people

Why Britons now trust weather forecasters

Nigel Farage’s stunts made him a successful politician. They won’t make him PM

Is AI writing taking over Westminster?

Some Scottish wrens are a striking case of “island syndrome”

Theresa May: Britain’s first—and last—woke prime minister

Europe has promised cash for defence. It’s failing to provide it

Marine Le Pen is running for the French presidency

NATO survives its bipolar summit

Poland and Ukraine have fallen out over history

Russians are growing anxious and angry

Europe’s public broadcasters go from prime time to hard-to-find

Texas is waging a battle to stop “Islamification”

What Graham Platner’s collapse means for the Democrats

Why Donald Trump won’t let a doomed bill die

Rise of the Gen-Z Luddite

Mehmet Oz, Donald Trump’s most effective TV star

The left’s common-sense problem on crime

Is Donald Trump serious in declaring the ceasefire with Iran over?

Rebuilding Syria will cost over $200bn. Bombs are not helping

Ahmed al-Sharaa is quashing Syria’s vast captagon trade

Can Africa take the Asian path to growth?

Brazilian cities are building vast surveillance networks

Mexico is placing an enormous bet on rail

Americans and Canadians are squabbling over mines

Can cutting-edge semiconductors supercharge Japan?

Laos has big dreams of exporting energy

Indian mangoes have been banned from Japan

How South Korean Buddhists are trying to stay relevant

What Indians are reading, and what that reveals about the self

Has China obtained the world’s most important machine?

China releases a prominent Christian pastor

A rare Chinese missile test unnerves Asian neighbours

China’s media gloat at Britain’s chaotic democracy

Child suspects are being prosecuted at younger ages

If ludicrous Trump flattery can save NATO, bring it on

A top Russian oligarch breaks the silence

Beware the top-heavy economy

Microsoft’s gaming strategy has misfired badly

Could a Chinese bike win the Tour de France?

China’s semiconductor industry is racing to catch the West’s

The secret to good questions

Elon Musk and the age of the corporate leviathan

China may struggle to fund Xi Jinping’s tech dreams

AI has taken over the stock market. The bond market is next

The World Bank has ditched its climate targets

Women’s progress at work is stalling

Three ways the LNG market could crack before winter

Europe’s economy is a mess. Its stock markets are a steal

An ageing society might not cost too much

The future of chipmaking looks more like Manhattan than Silicon Valley

How to hide from killer drones

A statistician’s guide to Wimbledon

How the biggest trees survive droughts

How little exercise can you get away with?

Inside the fantasy world of Donald Trump

A century on, Pilates flexes its muscles

Why Old Master paintings are back in vogue

The history of the human face is more than skin deep

The brightest tourism trend can be found in the darkest places

The real value of the Bayeux tapestry

Economic data, commodities and markets

Mona Khalil defied danger to protect her “family”

